As we have discussed, digital hardware uses the binary number system for its operations and data. Representing numbers and operations in binary form requires too many bits and needs lot of effort.  With octal, the bits are grouped in threes (because 23 = 8), and with hexadecimal, the binary bits are grouped in fours (because 24 = 16) and these groups are replaced with the respective octal or hexadecimal symbol. This conversion processes of binary numbers to octal and hexadecimal number systems   and vice versa are very easy. This short-hand notation is widely used in the design and operations of electronic circuits.

