The speed of a RAM refers to how fast the data in memory is accessed. It is measured in Mega Hertz (MHz). When a computer is in use, its RAM contains the following:

1. The operating system software.

2. The application software currently being used.

3. Any data that is being processed.

ii. Read Only Memory (ROM)

ROM is a permanent memory that can perform only read operations and its contents cannot be easily altered. ROM is non-volatile; the contents are retained even after the power is switched off. ROM is used in most computers to hold a small, special piece of 'boot up' program known as Basic Input Output System (BIOS).This software runs when the computer is switched on or 'boots up'. It checks the computer's hardware and then loads the operating system. It is slower than RAM. Figure 2.13 shows a typical ROM chip.

